From 9f80165c5f56020be6e209c915a61f6efc012739 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 22:44:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Generate whole strategies natively under
 HYPOTHESIS_ENGINE=hegel

Under the engine flag every draw still crossed into Python one primitive
at a time. Most built-in strategies are data, not code: their draw is a
function of their attributes and of the values drawn so far. hegel_ir.py
compiles that declarative core (integers, floats, booleans, binary, text,
lists, tuples, fixed dictionaries, one_of, sampled_from, just, map,
builds, shared, filter) into a tree mirroring each do_draw, and
hegel_gen.rs interprets it against the live libhegel case: spans, the
`many` continuation protocol, forced draws, the fixed-dictionary shuffle,
the filter and map retry rules, all as Hypothesis makes them, so the
choice sequence libhegel records is the one the Python engine would have
recorded. Anything else (composite, data, unique lists, recursion,
stateful, filtered sampled_from, optional keys, strategies that run
Python) is an opaque leaf: the interpreter hands it back to
ConjectureData.draw, which runs it interactively against the same case,
batching again underneath it. ConjectureData.draw consults the provider's
`draw_strategy` before a strategy's own do_draw, records the choices the
interpreter made, and lets a provider see the draws Hypothesis forces
(`draw_forced`, libhegel's new forced integer entry point) so the two
sequences stay aligned. The case's nodes are taken from libhegel's own
record. HYPOTHESIS_HEGEL_BATCH=0 keeps every draw interactive for
comparison.

The equivalence check: for each kind of strategy, a value generated
natively is exactly what the Python engine draws when it replays
libhegel's recorded choices through its own do_draw code.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
